        ":question/math-academy-id": "e-8505",
        ":question/problem": "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)"
